# models/coder1b/config.nsl
# NSL-Coder-1B Configuration
#
# Llama-3.2-1B-style shape with our 49152-token vocab. ~1.07B params.
# Sized to be the scale where FASE Deferred is load-bearing: tape AD OOMs
# on 16 GB VRAM, source-AD + FASE fits.

# Architecture
const VOCAB_SIZE = 49152
const D_MODEL = 2048
const N_LAYERS = 16
const N_HEADS = 32
const N_KV_HEADS = 8
const HEAD_DIM = 64
const D_FF = 8192
const MAX_SEQ_LEN = 2048
const ROPE_THETA = 500000.0
const DROPOUT = 0.0

# Pretrain hyperparameters — MUST agree with pretrain_prod.nsl; enforced by
# crates/nsl-cli/tests/pretrain_prod_agreement_gate.rs, which checks all THREE
# model sizes. The scheduler numbers are DERIVED from the real corpus:
#   PRETRAIN_TOTAL_STEPS = PRETRAIN_EPOCHS
#                        * floor(TRAIN_TOKENS / (MAX_SEQ_LEN * PRETRAIN_BATCH_SIZE))
#
# (Roadmap item 10. The previous 100000/2000 pair described a corpus this repo
# has never contained — the same fiction item 4 removed at 50M and item 9 at
# 500M. `PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUM` was renamed to the 50M/500M spelling
# `PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUMULATION` so ONE gate covers every size; nothing imported
# the old name.)
#
# The schedule deliberately MATCHES the 500M recipe where it can: 16,384
# tokens per optimizer step, 512 optimizer steps, the same corpus slice and the
# same held-out tail. batch 2 x seq 2048 = 4096 tokens per micro-step is four
# times the 500M micro-step, hence grad_accumulation 4 rather than 8.
# That makes the two runs comparable, NOT controlled: they also differ in
# DROPOUT (0.1 at 500M, 0.0 here) and ROPE_THETA (10000 vs 500000), and
# dropout is exactly the knob that moves a train-to-held-out gap. Do not read
# a VAL_LOSS difference between the sizes as a size effect.
#
# TOKEN BUDGET, stated plainly: 8.39M train tokens against ~1.07B params is
# ~0.008 tokens/param — a factor of ~2,550 short of a Chinchilla-ish
# ~20 tokens/param, and HALF the 500M recipe's ratio on the same corpus. This
# recipe validates the WORKFLOW at 1B — real corpus, real schedule,
# checkpoint/resume, held-out validation — it does not produce a well-trained
# 1B model. Fixing that needs a corpus, which is roadmap item 15.
const CORPUS_TOKENS = 8925916
const TRAIN_TOKENS = 8388608
const VAL_TOKENS = 524288
# LR AND WARMUP — THIS VALUE IS NOT MEASURED AT 1B. Read the provenance.
#
# This config carried lr = 3e-4, the same constant coder50m / coder500m /
# coder7b all carry across d_model 512 / 1280 / 2048 / 4096. That uniformity IS
# the defect: the value was never checked at any size until item 9 found it too
# high at 500M, where halving it was worth 0.57 nats of held-out loss.
#
# Item 10 tried to repeat that comparison at 1B rather than assume a 500M result
# transfers across a 1.6x width change. It ran two full epochs — 3e-4 and
# 1.5e-4 — and THE ARMS DID NOT SEPARATE INTO AN ANSWER:
#   - neither descends. armA's last-half train mean (9.408) is WORSE than its
#     first half (9.166); armB's is flat (9.162 -> 9.101).
#   - both land just under the ln(49152) = 10.803 uniform-predictor bound:
#     VAL 10.284 at 3e-4, 10.525 at 1.5e-4.
#   - the ordering FLIPS with the horizon (1.5e-4 leads early, trails on
#     held-out), one seed each, and no null control was ever obtained.
#   - worse: 1.5e-4's held-out number is driven by a late excursion that DID
#     NOT REPRODUCE when the same run was resumed from its own step-1200
#     checkpoint. The comparison rests on a value its own rerun contradicts.
# So the sign of "which LR is better at 1B" is not established, and this file
# must not launder either arm into a measured constant.
#
# The value below is therefore the WIDTH SCALING of the one LR result this repo
# does own — item 9's 1.5e-4 at d_model 1280 — carried to d_model 2048:
#   1.5e-4 * 1280/2048 = 9.4e-5, rounded to 1e-4.
# It is a principled default, not a measurement. The reason it cannot be
# validated here is TOKEN BUDGET, not tooling: see the note above — 0.008
# tokens/param cannot resolve a learning rate. Settling it needs the corpus
# roadmap item 15 provides.
#
# The arms, the corrected statistics, and what the earlier partial-window
# numbers got wrong are in models/benchmarks/PROD1B_VALIDATION_2026_08_19.md.
const PRETRAIN_LR = 0.0001
const PRETRAIN_WARMUP = 200
const PRETRAIN_TOTAL_STEPS = 2048
const PRETRAIN_MIN_LR = 0.00003
const PRETRAIN_BATCH_SIZE = 2
const PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUMULATION = 4
const PRETRAIN_WEIGHT_DECAY = 0.1
const PRETRAIN_BETA1 = 0.9
const PRETRAIN_BETA2 = 0.95
const PRETRAIN_EPOCHS = 1
const PRETRAIN_GRAD_CLIP = 1.0
const PRETRAIN_CHECKPOINT_EVERY = 100
# Shuffled, for the reason the 500M recipe records: a concatenated corpus
# should not be fed in file order, and item 8 made a seeded shuffle
# reproducible and resumable (it was entropy-keyed before). NOT measured to
# improve quality at 500M.
const PRETRAIN_SHUFFLE = 1

# Inference
const TOP_K = 40
const TEMPERATURE = 0.8
const MAX_GEN_TOKENS = 512

# Paths (relative to this directory). The train/val slices are a byte-exact
# prefix/tail cut of the shared corpus, materialized by
# models/benchmarks/make_prod_split.py — the same two files the 500M recipe
# reads, so the two sizes are scored on identical held-out text. The 13,020
# tokens between TRAIN_TOKENS and the start of the val tail are consumed by
# neither. Disjointness is automatic — they are separate files — so the gap
# buys SEPARATION: in a concatenated corpus the tokens just past the training
# prefix usually continue the same file.
const PRETRAIN_CORPUS = "../../data/tokens/train_new.bin"
const PRETRAIN_DATA = "../../data/tokens/prod_train_slice.bin"
const PRETRAIN_VAL_DATA = "../../data/tokens/prod_val_slice.bin"
const CHECKPOINT_DIR = "checkpoints"

# Parameter budget (approximate, f32):
#   Embedding (tied LM head): 49152 × 2048 =   100.66M
#   Per TransformerBlock:
#     GQA Q:                  2048 × 2048 =     4.19M
#     GQA K:                  2048 ×  512 =     1.05M
#     GQA V:                  2048 ×  512 =     1.05M
#     GQA O:                  2048 × 2048 =     4.19M
#     SwiGLU (gate+up+down):  3 × (2048×8192) = 50.33M
#     Block total:                          ≈   60.81M
#   16 blocks:                              ≈    972.9M
#   Final RMSNorm:                          ≈         0
#   TOTAL:                                  ≈   1074M  (~1.07B)
